<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oxford Movement</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1830s</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Tractarianism, Tractarians</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> Movement was a high-church movement within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a>, begun in the 1830s by clergy mostly associated with the University of Oxford, which developed into Anglo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>. Prompted by the Irish Church Temporalities Bill and John Keble's Assize Sermon of 1833, its leaders, among them Keble, John Henry Newman and Edward Bouverie Pusey, published the Tracts for the Times, arguing that the church's authority came from God through apostolic succession rather than from the state. Newman's reception into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> in 1845 shook the movement, yet it led to Anglican religious orders, a more central place for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucharist">Eucharist</a> and the wider use of vestments.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oxford-movement.jpg" alt="Oxford Movement" width="330" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rosa Corder · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rosa_Corder_(1853-1893)_-_Reverend_Edward_Bouverie_Pusey,_Member_of_Keble_College_Council_(1870–1880)_(copy_of_an_earlier_work)_-_PCF31_-_Keble_College.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In the early nineteenth century many of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a>'s senior clergy saw themselves as latitudinarian, or liberal, while many parish clergy were Evangelicals shaped by the revival led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-wesley">John Wesley</a>. The Irish Church Temporalities Bill of 1833 provided for merging dioceses and provinces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-ireland">Church of Ireland</a> and for ending the vestry assessment, a grievance in the Tithe War. Some politicians and clergy feared it would lead to similar attacks on the Church of England, and eventually to its disestablishment and the loss of its endowments. Besides Keble, Newman and Pusey, the leading Tractarians included Charles Marriott, Richard Froude, Robert Wilberforce, Isaac Williams and William Palmer; all but Williams and Palmer were fellows of Oriel College.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Tractarians held the Branch Theory, by which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglicanism">Anglicanism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodoxy">Eastern Orthodoxy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> were three branches of the historic undivided church, and they wanted parts of medieval liturgy restored to a church they thought had become too plain. Sold for pennies and widely distributed, the Tracts drew wide attention to these views. In the last of them, Tract 90, Newman argued that Catholic doctrine as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-trent">Council of Trent</a> defined it was compatible with the Thirty-Nine Articles. The group also translated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-fathers">Church Fathers</a> as the Library of the Fathers, 48 volumes mainly edited by Marriott, and it produced the hymnbook Hymns Ancient and Modern of 1861. Opponents called its members Newmanites and Puseyites.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Critics called the movement a Romanising tendency and accused it of secrecy. Newman's reception into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a>, followed by that of Henry Edward Manning in 1851, called into question its middle way between Low Church Anglicanism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>, and many others it had influenced, among them Gerard Manley Hopkins and Augustus Pugin, also became Catholics. Ritual changes led to court cases in the dispute over ritualism. Many Tractarian priests worked in slums, partly because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> refused them livings, and their critique of social policy fed into the Christian Social Union, which debated the just wage, renting, infant mortality and industrial conditions. Anglo-Catholicism went on to shape Anglicanism around the world.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oxford Movement” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Neale belonged to the Tractarian <b>Oxford Movement</b> and wished to bring Catholic ceremony, saints' days and music back into the Anglican church; the gift let him use medieval melodies for new Anglican hymns.</blockquote><a href="/songs/good-king-wenceslas">Good King Wenceslas</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hymnal Noted, made up entirely of Latin hymns in English with music drawn mainly from plainchant, was a product of the <b>Oxford Movement</b> in the Church of England, and the hymn was taken up for its medieval air and its fit to the season.</blockquote><a href="/songs/o-come-o-come-emmanuel">O Come, O Come, Emmanuel</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1834 John Keble, one of the leaders of the <b>Oxford Movement</b> in the Church of England, translated it into English as &quot;Hail, Gladdening Light&quot;, keeping the irregular metre of the original.</blockquote><a href="/songs/phos-hilaron">Phos Hilaron (O Gladsome Light)</a></li></ul></section>
